\description{
Twelve indicators from the IMF's World Economic Outlook (WEO) database of
October 2026, laid out tidy. One row is one entity in one year, and each
variable has its own column. An entity is one of 197 countries or one of 13
IMF country groups, and the years run from 2000 to 2031, so the panel has
6,720 rows.
}
\details{
The columns cover what a student of international economics asks first:
how fast an economy grows, how fast its prices rise, who has work, what it
earns from and pays to the rest of the world, how its people live, and what
its government takes in, spends and owes. \link{weo_full} has the IMF's other 32
indicators, and \link{weo_indicators} says what each one measures. \link{weo_mini} is
twelve rows of this table that fit on one screen.

The WEO mixes actual data and IMF projections in one row. \code{latest_actual}
marks where the actual data stop. For most countries the fiscal numbers are
actual through 2025, and from 2026 on almost every value is an IMF
projection.

Net interest is not a column. It is one line of arithmetic,
\code{primary_balance - overall_balance}: the interest the government pays minus
the interest it receives, in percent of GDP. For Japan in 2025 that is
-0.511 minus -0.709, which is 0.198.

To tell a group from a country, look at the code. An IMF group's code
starts with G and then a digit or an X (\code{G110}); a country's second
character is a letter (Ghana is \code{GHA}).
}
\section{Vintage}{

The IMF's World Economic Outlook database of October 2026. This table is a
frozen snapshot of that release: it does not change when the IMF publishes a
new WEO or revises these numbers.
}

\section{Transformations}{

The first nine columns come from the IMF's workbook \code{WEOOct2026all.xlsx}, in
two steps. First, an extract stacked its Countries and Country Groups sheets,
kept 13 of 44 indicators, the years 2000 to 2031 and 9 of 29 descriptive
columns, and wrote each number in its shortest form. Second,
\code{data-raw/weo_panel.R} kept four indicators, gave them plain names, reshaped
them to one row per entity and year, and rounded them to three decimals. It
also added two columns from the IMF's labels. \code{fiscal_year} is the IMF's own
label. \code{latest_actual} turns the IMF's label, such as FY2024/25, into one
calendar year, following the rule in the IMF's notes for that country.

The last eight columns, added in version 0.3.0, come from the same release
downloaded with the \href{https://github.com/Teal-Insights/r-imfweo}{imfweo}
package, given plain names and rounded to three decimals. \code{data-raw/weo.R}
checks the download against the workbook, cell by cell, for every indicator
they share: no number differs. It also checks that revenue minus
expenditure equals the overall balance for every country and year, to
rounding. No value in the first nine columns changed.
}

